        <!-- Section 1 -->
        <div id="why-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why Granite Needs to Be Sealed</h2>

          <p>
            Despite its reputation as nearly indestructible, granite is more vulnerable than most homeowners realize. The stone forms from cooled magma deep underground, and its crystalline structure includes microscopic gaps between mineral grains. These gaps are what give granite its visual depth and natural beauty — but they also create pathways for liquids to penetrate the surface. When you spill red wine, coffee, olive oil, or anything else on unsealed granite, capillary action can pull the liquid into the stone within minutes. Once below the surface, the staining compound is essentially permanent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealers solve this by filling the microscopic gaps with a hydrophobic resin that prevents liquid absorption. The <strong>best granite sealer</strong> products work by penetrating into the stone rather than coating the surface — they create the protective barrier within the structure of the granite itself. This penetrating approach has critical advantages over surface coatings: there's no film to scratch, no buildup over multiple applications, and no change to the visual appearance of the stone.
          </p>

          <p>
            How often you need to seal depends on the porosity of your specific granite. Dense, fine-grained granites like absolute black or blue pearl may need sealing only every 5+ years (or never). Coarse, porous varieties like Brazilian white or honed travertine may need sealing every 6-12 months. The water bead test (covered in section 5) is the definitive way to determine your specific stone's needs.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Granite Sealing Reality Check</p>
