                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Premium sealer lifespan</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 2 -->
        <div id="sealer-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Three Types of Granite Sealer Explained</h2>

          <p>
            Granite sealers fall into three functional categories. The differences matter because choosing the wrong type for your specific stone and use case wastes both money and effort. Here's a clear breakdown:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Penetrating Impregnators</h3>
          <p>
            The right answer for 95% of granite countertops. Impregnators carry hydrophobic resins (typically silanes, siloxanes, or fluoropolymers) deep into the stone where they bond with the mineral structure. The resulting protection is invisible — no shine, no haze, no color change. Examples include Miracle 511, Tenax Hydrex Plus, and StoneTech BulletProof. Lifespan ranges from 1-5 years depending on product quality and stone porosity.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Color-Enhancing Sealers</h3>
          <p>
            A subset of impregnators that also include color-enhancing resins to deepen the visual appearance of the stone. The protection mechanism is similar to standard impregnators, but the resins change how light refracts through the granite surface. The effect is most dramatic on darker granites with subtle patterns and on honed (matte) finishes. <strong>The color change is permanent</strong>, so always test before committing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Topical Sealers (Generally Avoid)</h3>
          <p>
            Topical sealers create a film on the surface of the granite rather than penetrating into it. Acrylic and polyurethane formulations dominate this category. They're cheap and provide instant water resistance, but they have significant downsides: visible film that can scratch and yellow, buildup with each reapplication, and incompatibility with most kitchen cleaners. Topical sealers have legitimate uses on granite floor tile in low-traffic areas but are inappropriate for countertops.
          </p>

