        <!-- Section 4: How to Seal -->
        <div id="how-to-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How to Seal Granite Countertops (Step-by-Step)</h2>

          <p>
            Sealing granite is one of the easier home maintenance tasks — but small mistakes lead to disappointing results. Follow this five-step process and you'll get professional-quality protection on the first attempt.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Clean the Surface Thoroughly</h3>
          <p>
            Any contamination on the surface — cooking oil residue, soap film, food particles — will block sealer absorption. Use a pH-neutral stone cleaner (not generic kitchen cleaners, which are typically alkaline and can degrade the existing stone). Granite Gold Daily Cleaner or Method Daily Granite are good options. Spray, wipe with a microfiber cloth, and rinse with clean water on a fresh cloth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Allow Complete Drying</h3>
          <p>
            Sealers cannot penetrate damp stone. Wait at least <strong>24 hours</strong> after cleaning before applying sealer. The granite must be visibly dry and feel dry to the touch. In humid climates or after deep cleaning, extend this to 48 hours. Skipping this step is the #1 cause of disappointing sealing results.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Apply the Sealer</h3>
          <p>
            Pour a small amount of sealer onto a clean, lint-free cloth or apply directly with a spray bottle if your product is spray-formulated. Work in 2x2-foot sections, applying enough product that the granite surface stays visibly wet for the full absorption period (typically 5-10 minutes; check your product instructions). Don't pool the product or over-apply — a thin, even wet film is the goal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Wipe Off All Excess</h3>
          <p>
            This is the step that separates good results from hazy, streaky failures. After the absorption period, take a clean dry cloth and wipe off ALL residue from the surface. Any sealer remaining on top of the stone will cure into a hazy film that requires aggressive cleaning to remove. Wipe in overlapping circular motions, checking carefully for any spots that look glossy or smeared.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Allow Full Cure</h3>
          <p>
            Most granite sealers reach initial water resistance within 1-2 hours, but full chemical cure takes 24-48 hours. Avoid placing food directly on the surface, using harsh cleaners, or wiping with abrasive sponges during the cure period. After full curing, your granite is protected for 1-5 years depending on the product and stone porosity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 5: Water Test -->
        <div id="water-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Water Bead Test: When to Reseal</h2>

          <p>
            Knowing when to reseal is just as important as knowing how. The water bead test is the definitive answer for any granite countertop, regardless of the manufacturer's recommended schedule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pour about a tablespoon of water onto your granite countertop in several locations — heavily used areas near the sink and stove, less-used areas near the corners. Watch the water for 10-15 minutes.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>Water beads up and stays beaded:</strong> Sealer is performing perfectly. No action needed.</li>
            <li><strong>Water flattens but doesn't absorb:</strong> Sealer is still working but weakening. Plan to reseal within 6-12 months.</li>
            <li><strong>Water absorbs and creates a dark spot within 1-15 minutes:</strong> Time to reseal now.</li>
            <li><strong>Water absorbs and creates a dark spot within 1 minute:</strong> Surface is unprotected. Reseal urgently.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The dark spot from water absorption isn't permanent — it disappears as the water evaporates over a few hours. But the same absorption with red wine, coffee, or oil <em>will</em> create a permanent stain. The water test is your warning system.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section: Cleaning -->
        <div class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5b. How to Clean Sealed Granite the Right Way</h2>

          <p>
            The single fastest way to destroy a perfectly sealed granite countertop is using the wrong cleaner. Most generic kitchen cleaners are alkaline and contain ingredients that strip impregnator sealers from the stone over time. Within months of regular use, your protection erodes and you're back to staining risk despite having sealed the surface. Cleaning protocol matters as much as sealer selection.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Avoid:</strong> Generic kitchen cleaners (Windex, 409, Mr. Clean, Lysol, etc.), bleach-based products, ammonia-based products, vinegar, lemon juice, abrasive scrubs, and anything labeled as a degreaser. All of these chemistries either strip the sealer, etch the stone surface, or both. Acidic cleaners are particularly damaging because granite contains calcite minerals that react with acids over time, creating dull spots that can't be polished out without professional restoration.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use instead:</strong> A dedicated stone cleaner with neutral pH (Granite Gold, Method Daily Granite, Bona Stone) or simply warm water with a few drops of dish soap. Wipe with a microfiber cloth, never an abrasive sponge. For stuck-on food, let the cleaner dwell for a minute before wiping rather than scrubbing aggressively. The microfiber cloth handles 95% of cleaning tasks; reserve a soft nylon brush for textured edges or detail areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyday wipe-downs after cooking, a 50/50 mixture of rubbing alcohol and water in a spray bottle works exceptionally well. The alcohol disinfects the surface without damaging the sealer, dries quickly without streaks, and costs almost nothing. Many professional fabricators recommend this DIY solution over branded daily cleaners. Apply, wipe with microfiber, done in 30 seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            For dried-on spills, never use scrapers, steel wool, or magic erasers — all three can scratch the polished surface of granite even though the stone itself is hard enough to resist normal wear. A plastic putty knife held at a low angle is the safe option for stuck-on food residue, followed by a regular cleaning pass with stone cleaner and microfiber.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spot-cleaning oil-based stains (cooking oil, butter splatters) requires a different approach than water-based stains. Oil stains develop slowly into the stone and can leave permanent dark patches even on sealed surfaces if neglected. Address them immediately with a poultice paste — flour mixed with hydrogen peroxide for light granites, or flour with acetone for darker stones. Apply the paste, cover with plastic wrap taped down at the edges, and leave for 24 hours. The poultice draws the oil out of the stone as it dries, often completely removing fresh stains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 6: Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">6. The Five Most Common Granite Sealing Mistakes</h2>

          <p>
            We've helped readers troubleshoot dozens of failed sealing projects over the years. The same five mistakes account for the vast majority of poor results. Avoid these and your sealing project will succeed.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Wet or Damp Granite</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealers are hydrophobic by design — they're literally engineered to repel water. Applying them to a damp surface means the sealer cannot penetrate because water is already occupying the pore structure. The product sits on the surface, doesn't bond, and creates a hazy film when it dries. Always wait at least 24 hours after cleaning before sealing, and longer in humid conditions or after deep cleaning.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Failing to Wipe Off Residue</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The most common visual failure with granite sealing is hazy, streaky residue across the countertop surface. This happens when excess sealer that didn't penetrate is left on the surface to cure. Once cured, this residue requires aggressive cleaning with a stone-safe stripper to remove. Always wipe off all visible product within the manufacturer's specified window — typically 5-15 minutes after application.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Wrong Sealer Type</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Topical acrylic sealers labeled for natural stone can be acceptable on shower walls or floor tile, but they're inappropriate for kitchen countertops. The film visible on the surface scratches with cutting boards and pots, yellows over time, and reacts poorly with food acids. Use only penetrating impregnators on countertops, and reserve enhancers for stones that genuinely need color intensification.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Stone That Doesn't Need It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some granites are naturally non-porous — absolute black, blue pearl, and certain other dense varieties may not need sealing at all. Applying sealer to these stones is wasteful and can cause subtle haze if the product can't penetrate. Always perform the water bead test first. If water beads on the unsealed surface for 15+ minutes, your stone may not need sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Testing Color Enhancers Before Application</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Color-enhancing sealers permanently change the appearance of granite. Some homeowners love the deeper, richer look. Others find that their granite looks too dark or too busy after enhancement. Always test on a hidden area — under-sink cutout, backsplash piece behind the stove, scrap piece left from installation. Live with the test for 24 hours under different lighting conditions before committing to full application.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best granite sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Miracle 511 Impregnator is the best overall granite sealer for most homeowners. It penetrates deep into the stone, lasts 3-5 years between applications, and doesn't change the appearance of the granite. For water-based application without solvent odor, Tenax Hydrex Plus is the best alternative. For maximum protection on highly porous stones, StoneTech BulletProof offers a 5-year warranty.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I seal my granite?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most granite countertops need resealing every 1-3 years depending on stone porosity and product quality. Premium impregnators like Miracle 511 last 3-5 years. The water bead test is the definitive answer: if water no longer beads on the surface within 10 minutes, it's time to reseal. Over-sealing doesn't damage the stone, but under-sealing leads to permanent stains.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is granite sealer food-safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most modern granite sealers are food-safe once fully cured (24-48 hours after application). Look for products that specifically state "food-safe" or "NSF-certified" for kitchen use. Miracle 511, Granite Gold, and Tenax Hydrex Plus are all food-safe after curing. Avoid food contact with the surface during the cure period.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I apply granite sealer myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, granite sealing is a beginner-friendly DIY task that takes 15-30 minutes for a typical kitchen. The application process is simple: clean the granite, allow 24 hours of drying, apply sealer with a clean cloth, allow 5-10 minutes for absorption, then wipe off all residue. The most common mistake is failing to remove excess product, which causes hazing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing change the look of my granite?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard penetrating impregnators do not visibly change granite. They're designed to be invisible — no shine, no haze, no color shift. Only color-enhancing sealers (like Miracle Mira) intentionally deepen color, and that change is permanent. Always read product descriptions carefully if you want to preserve the original appearance of your stone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if I don't seal my granite?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most granite varieties will gradually develop staining from regular kitchen use — water marks near the sink, oil shadows around the stove, dark spots from spilled wine or coffee. Some staining is removable with poultice treatments, but deep stains may be permanent. Sealing is inexpensive insurance against permanent damage to a costly countertop.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
